const faq = [
  { q: 'How do ravens remember where they hid food?', a: 'Ravens use spatial memory — encoding the precise location of each cache relative to landmarks — along with memory of what was cached and when (episodic-like memory). Studies show they can relocate caches accurately days to weeks later, distinguish more perishable from less perishable items, and prioritise recovering perishable caches first.' },
  { q: 'Can other ravens steal hidden food?', a: 'Yes, and this is a major driver of raven caching behaviour. Ravens observe each other and regularly steal caches (called "pilfering"). A raven that sees another caching will return later to steal from that specific location. In response, cachers have developed counter-strategies: re-caching after being watched, caching behind barriers, and using deceptive "false caching" movements.' },
  { q: 'How many caches does a raven make?', a: 'Individual ravens can make hundreds to thousands of caches over the course of a year. The exact number depends on food availability — a raven at a large carcass in winter may make dozens of caches in a single session. Each cache is typically a single food item buried in the ground or wedged in a crevice.' },
];
